        "fontSize": "large"
      },
      "title": "I5 hint 1"
    },
    "viz_i5_h2": {
      "type": "splunk.markdown",
      "options": {
        "markdown": "# HINT 2 — Investigation 5\n\nRefresh: REQUEST != GRANT; ALLOW != EXECUTION; DENY != automatic proof of non-execution; missing event != blocked; Splunk != enforcement; ATTACK != ALERT; 0 DET-MCP-001 rows != SAFE; HEC success != searchable evidence.\n\nPath A is still Search. This is not the full solution.\n",
        "fontColor": "#17202A",
        "backgroundColor": "#FFFFFF",
        "fontSize": "large"
      },
      "title": "I5 hint 2"
    },
    "viz_i5_sol": {
      "type": "splunk.markdown",
      "options": {
        "markdown": "# Solution — Investigation 5 What can you prove?\n\nThis is **Path B — show solution**. Open it only after you tried Path A in Search. It is an answer key, not policy. Splunk does not enforce. Path A remains Search with your LIVE run.id.\n\n**SOLUTION SPL** (`Q-MCP-AFTER-DENY`)\n\nCopy this into Search. Replace `$run_id$` with the LIVE UUID, or leave the token for Evidence to investigate REPLAY.\n\n```\nindex=agentsec_telemetry sourcetype=otel:agentic:json earliest=0 \"agentsec.run.id\"=\"$run_id$\" (\"event.name\"=agentsec.control.decision OR \"event.name\"=agentsec.mcp.started OR \"event.name\"=agentsec.mcp.completed OR \"event.name\"=agentsec.mcp.failed)\n| eval run_id=mvindex(mvdedup('agentsec.run.id'),0)\n| eval tool=mvindex(mvdedup('gen_ai.tool.name'),0)\n| eval event_name=mvindex(mvdedup('event.name'),0)\n| eval decision=mvindex(mvdedup('agentsec.control.decision'),0)\n| eval sequence=tonumber(mvindex(mvdedup('agentsec.sequence'),0))\n| eval is_deny=if(event_name=\"agentsec.control.decision\" AND decision=\"DENY\",1,0)\n| eval is_mcp=if(match(event_name,\"^agentsec\\\\.mcp\\\\.\"),1,0)\n| eventstats min(eval(if(is_deny=1,sequence,null()))) as deny_seq, max(is_deny) as has_deny by run_id, tool\n| where has_deny=1 AND is_mcp=1 AND sequence>deny_seq\n| table run_id, tool, deny_seq, sequence, event_name, decision\n```\n\n**WHY THESE STAGES**\n\n- **Why this hunt?** It asks whether mcp.started followed DENY for the same run/tool.\n- **Why zero rows are not a detector?** No notable. Completeness first.\n- DET-MCP-001 is the disabled saved search for the same invariant. It is not an enabled operational detection.\n\n**EXPECTED RESULT SHAPE**\n\nClassification exercise. Q-MCP-AFTER-DENY on a complete defended RETEST copy is 0 rows — corroboration, not independent prevention proof. DET-MCP-001 is silent on fail-open ALLOW and on DENY-without-start.\n\n**WHAT YOU ARE SEEING**\n\nQ-MCP-AFTER-DENY asks whether mcp.started followed DENY on the same run/tool. Zero rows on a complete copy means that sequence was not indexed. The DETECT tab positive-control table is SIMULATED.\n\n**WHAT IT MEANS**\n\nYou can prove what the complete evidence says. You cannot promote gaps, HEC 200, Studio rendering, or detector silence into prevention.\n\n**WHAT IT DOES NOT MEAN**\n\nThis workshop does not prove INV-001 for other tools, production IAM, or later MCP labs. Zero detection rows are not a new detector.\n\n**SECURITY CONNECTION**\n\nControl `CTRL-MCP-001` · invariant `INV-001` · hunt `Q-MCP-AFTER-DENY`. Splunk does **not** ALLOW or DENY.\n\n**NEXT CHALLENGE**\n\nMCP-I6-ATTACK-VS-RETEST\n",
        "fontColor": "#17202A",
        "backgroundColor": "#FFFFFF",
        "fontSize": "large"
      },
      "title": "I5 solution"
    },
