    "viz_nb2_limits": {
      "type": "splunk.markdown",
      "options": {
        "markdown": "**YOUR OBSERVATION.** Which execution events exist for this run, and what does their presence or absence let you say? Not saved by this page.\n\n**WHAT THE EVIDENCE SUPPORTS.** `agentsec.mcp.started` is handler start. `agentsec.mcp.completed` is success after a start. `agentsec.mcp.failed` is a start followed by an error. `agentsec.pipeline.stopped` means the run was halted before the handler.\n\n**WHAT THIS DOES NOT PROVE.** An absent `mcp.started` in Splunk is corroboration of non-execution, not proof: this is an indexed copy, and a missing event can mean a missing copy. The runtime handler count in the lab is the authoritative source. **CONTROL DECISION != EXECUTION.**\n\n**NEXT.** Question 3 below: what was requested, and was it granted?\n",
        "fontColor": "#17202A",
        "backgroundColor": "#FFFFFF",
        "fontSize": "large"
      }
    },
    "viz_nb3_q": {
      "type": "splunk.markdown",
      "options": {
        "markdown": "# 3 of 5 — What was requested, and was it granted?\n\n**QUESTION.** What capability did the agent request, and was it inside the scope the server granted?\n\n**WHY THIS MATTERS.** An agent may know a tool exists without being authorized to use it. Catalogue visibility and authority are different things.\n\n**EVIDENCE RESULT.** Read the table below. It reads the CURRENT EVIDENCE run, LIVE or REPLAY, as stated at the top. Compare the requested scope with the allowed scope.\n",
        "fontColor": "#17202A",
        "backgroundColor": "#FFFFFF",
        "fontSize": "large"
      }
    },
    "viz_nb3_r": {
      "type": "splunk.table",
      "title": "EVIDENCE RESULT — requested scope vs granted scope (OBSERVED)",
